Notebook[{Cell["Transversely polarized proton-proton classes (A_TT), step 4: \
the NLO hard coefficient of one class", "Title"], 
  Cell["Input: Results/<route>/class-<n>/Algebra.wl and Reduced.wl (steps 2 \
and 3), whose Born table of transversely polarized 2 -> 2 squares replaces \
the table of Processes/PartonicBorn.  Output: \
Results/<route>/class-<n>/Transverse.wl with the finite hard coefficient of \
A_TT in the basis {regular, D0, D1, delta} and its comparison with the \
Fortran code of Mukherjee, Stratmann and Vogelsang \
(Reference/pp-transverse-fortran.wl).  The structure is that of \
Processes/Hadronic/4-Unpolarized.wl (read that first): the same threshold \
expansion, virtual normalization, coupling renormalization, convolution \
formulas for the two incoming legs and the fragmenting parton, pole \
cancellation and finite coefficient.\n\nThe hard coefficient is sigma(s,v,w) \
= s E dsigma/d^3p of the tagged parton (docs/CONVENTIONS.md), t = -s(1-v), u \
= -s v w, s4 = s v (1-w), here for the transverse double-spin difference: \
delta sigma_TT = (1/2)[sigma(up,up) - sigma(up,down)] projected on cos(2 Phi) \
(step 2: the projection TT = (XX - NN)/2),\n\n   delta sigma_TT = alphaS^2 \
b(v) delta(1-w) + (alphaS^3/Pi) [ R(v,w)/w + B(v) D0(w) + C(v) D1(w) + A(v) \
delta(1-w) ] + O(alphaS^4),\n\nwith the Born b = |M|^2_TT/(gs^4 s v) [at D = \
4], the distributions D0 = [1/(1-w)]_+ and D1 = [log(1-w)/(1-w)]_+ defined at \
w = 1.  Scales: muR (renormalization), muF (transversity distributions of \
both incoming hadrons), muFF (fragmentation).\n\nWhat differs from the spin \
average (docs/TRANSVERSE-PP.md): (1) the HVBM scheme, whose hatted invariants \
step 3 has turned into integrals in D + 2 dimensions, and the \
four-dimensional spin vectors; (2) the collinear counterterms of the two \
incoming legs use the transversity kernel Delta_T P_qq(z) = CF [2 z/(1-z)_+ + \
3/2 delta(1-z)] (SplittingKernel[\"Transverse qq\"]) with no mixing (a gluon \
has no transversity, and the kernel connects a quark only to the same quark), \
so each incoming counterterm is the kernel convoluted with the class's own TT \
Born; (3) the fragmenting parton is unpolarized: the unpolarized kernels with \
the TT Born table (class 12: P_gg x Born(q qb -> g)_TT and P_gq x [Born(q qb \
-> q)_TT + Born(q qb -> qb)_TT + 2 (Nf-1) Born(q qb -> q')_TT]; class 7, \
without Born term: P_gq x Born(q q -> q)_TT only); (4) there is no finite \
helicity-restoring term (it belongs to the longitudinal case); (5) the \
evanescent Phi-independent projection \"Symmetric\" of step 2 is carried \
through the same steps as a check: its poles must cancel too, and its finite \
part is displayed.", "Text"], Cell["Load", "Section"], 
  Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"..\", \"..\", \"FeynFacet\", \
\"FeynFacet.wl\"}]]", "Input"], Cell["route = \"FeynCalc\";\nclass = \
\"5\";\nspin = \"TT\";\nalgebra = LoadResult[\"Algebra\", route <> \
\"/class-\" <> class];\nreduced = LoadResult[\"Reduced\", route <> \
\"/class-\" <> class];\nspec = \
algebra[\"Spec\"];\nGet[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"classes.wl\"}]];\nregion = s > 0 && muR > 0 && muF > 0 && muFF > 0 && 0 < v \
< 1 && 0 < w < 1 && Nf > 0;\ntoVW = {t -> -s (1 - v), s4 -> s v (1 - w), u -> \
-s v w};\ncouplings = {gs -> Sqrt[4 Pi alphaS]};\n(* the transversely \
polarized Born table of step 2 (|M|^2/gs^4 in s, t, D for the projection \
being assembled); a channel absent from the table is looked up as\n   its \
charge conjugate (every quark <-> antiquark, the same transverse square), as \
in Processes/Hadronic; a channel absent in both forms is zero \
*)\nconjugateName[name_] := StringReplace[name, {\"qpb\" -> \"qp\", \"qp\" -> \
\"qpb\", \"qb\" -> \"q\", \"q\" -> \"qb\"}];\nbornOf[a_, b_, c_, projection_: \
spin] := Module[{name = a <> \" \" <> b <> \" -> \" <> c, table = \
algebra[\"BornTable\"][projection], candidates},\n  candidates = \
Select[{name, conjugateName[name]}, KeyExistsQ[table, #] &];\n  If[candidates \
=== {}, 0, table[First[candidates]]]];\nhard[square_] := square (4 Pi \
alphaS)^2/(16 Pi^2 s v);        (* the hard cross section of a Born square \
|M|^2/gs^4 *)\nspec[\"Name\"]", "Input"], Cell["Born", "Section"], 
  Cell["The TT Born hard part through order eps (the order-eps term \
multiplies the poles of the counterterms).  For the symmetric projection the \
order-eps^0 part vanishes (step 2) and only the order-eps part remains.", 
   "Text"], Cell["bornHard = If[spec[\"Born\"] === None, 0, \
Series[hard[algebra[\"Born\"][spin]/gs^4] /. D -> 4 - 2 eps /. toVW, {eps, 0, \
1}]];\nborn0 = If[bornHard === 0, 0, Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[bornHard, \
{eps, 0, 0}]]];\nborn1 = If[bornHard === 0, 0, \
Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[bornHard, {eps, 0, 1}]]];\nsymmetricBorn = \
If[spec[\"Born\"] === None, {0, 0}, With[{ser = \
Series[hard[algebra[\"Born\"][\"Symmetric\"]/gs^4] /. D -> 4 - 2 eps /. toVW, \
{eps, 0, 1}]},\n  Simplify /@ {SeriesCoefficient[ser, {eps, 0, 0}], \
SeriesCoefficient[ser, {eps, 0, 1}]}]];\nRequire[symmetricBorn[[1]] === 0, \
\"The symmetric projection of the Born term does not vanish at eps = 0\", \
symmetricBorn];\n{born0, born1, \"symmetric Born: eps x \" -> \
symmetricBorn[[2]]}", "Input"], 
  Cell["Real emission and its threshold expansion", "Section"], 
  Cell["As in Processes/Hadronic: the integrated real contribution contains \
s4^(-eps) and, from the unmixed families, z^(-1-eps) and z^(-eps) with z \
proportional to s4 = s v (1-w); ThresholdSplit makes every power of (1-w) \
explicit and EndpointExpand expands the branches (1-w)^(-1-lambda eps) into \
the distributions.  The measure of the two unobserved partons gives 1/(32 \
Pi^3); gs^6 -> (4 Pi alphaS)^3 with one factor muR^(2 eps) kept for the extra \
coupling.  The same expansion is done for the symmetric projection.", 
   "Text"], Cell["realHardOf[projection_] := muR^(2 eps) (4 Pi alphaS)^3 \
reduced[\"Real\"][projection]/gs^6/(32 Pi^3) /. \
toVW;\nexpandReal[projection_] := Module[{split = \
ThresholdSplit[realHardOf[projection], w, eps]},\n  \
EndpointExpand[split[\"Regular\"], split[\"Branches\"], eps, w, region] /. \
reduced[\"ThresholdValues\"]];   (* the inert master functions at w = 1 are \
numbers *)\nrealExpanded = expandReal[spin];\nsymmetricReal = \
expandReal[\"Symmetric\"];\nSimplify[realExpanded[\"Pole2\"], region]", 
   "Input"], Cell["Virtual correction", "Section"], 
  Cell["The interference of step 2 with the loop masters of step 3 inserted.  \
Its normalization follows from the conventions, not from the poles.  FeynArts \
(through FCFAConvert) writes the one-loop amplitude as M1 = integral d^D \
loop/(2 Pi)^D of a sum of terms c_j x (propagators), with every factor i of \
the vertices and propagators and the sign of a closed quark loop already in \
c_j; the overall phase convention of FeynArts is the same for the tree and \
the loop amplitude and cancels in M1 M0*.  The scalar integrals I_j of steps \
2 and 3 are defined with the measure d^D loop/(i Pi^(D/2)), and\n\n   d^D \
loop/(2 Pi)^D = (i Pi^(D/2)/(2 Pi)^D) d^D loop/(i Pi^(D/2)) = (i/(4 \
Pi)^(D/2)) d^D loop/(i Pi^(D/2)),   so   M1 = (i/(4 Pi)^(D/2)) Sum_j c_j \
I_j.\n\nStep 2 stores virtual = Sum_j [i c_j M0*] I_j (the spin sums make \
each i c_j M0* real; step 3 takes the real parts of the I_j in the physical \
region), hence\n\n   2 Re(M1 M0 = (2/(4 Pi)^(D/2)) x virtual = (2/(4 Pi)^2) \
(4 Pi)^eps x virtual,\n\nwith phase +1, set explicitly below.  Two \
independent checks follow: here, the double pole of the virtual correction \
must be the universal -(alphaS/(2 Pi)) (C_a + C_b + C_c + C_d) times the Born \
term, C = CF for a quark and CA for a gluon (Catani, Phys. Lett. B 427 (1998) \
161); in the section Pole cancellation, it must cancel the double pole of the \
real emission.  The ratio of the two double poles is still computed and must \
be 2/(4 Pi)^2 up to a phase.\n\nThe same normalization multiplies the \
symmetric projection.", "Text"], Cell["virtualRawOf[projection_] := \
If[spec[\"Virtual\"], (4 Pi alphaS)^3 muR^(2 eps) \
reduced[\"Virtual\"][projection]/gs^6/(16 Pi^2 s v) /. toVW, 0];\nvirtualRaw \
= virtualRawOf[spin];\nvirtualSeries = If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, \
Series[virtualRaw, {eps, 0, 0}]];\nvirtualPole2Raw = If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, \
Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[virtualSeries, {eps, 0, -2}], \
region]];\nloopNormalization = If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, 2/(4 Pi)^2 (4 \
Pi)^eps];          (* 2/(4 Pi)^(D/2) with D/2 = 2 - eps, phase +1 (derivation \
above) *)\n(* the ratio of the two double poles (\"Pole2\" is the coefficient \
of delta(1-w)/eps^2) must be the constant 2/(4 Pi)^2 of the loop measure, up \
to a phase;\n   the SU(N) relation CF = (CA^2-1)/(2 CA) is needed to see it.  \
The phase itself is tested by the cancellation of the double poles below \
*)\npoleRatio = If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, \
Simplify[-realExpanded[\"Pole2\"]/virtualPole2Raw /. CF -> (CA^2 - 1)/(2 CA), \
region]];\nRequire[virtualRaw === 0 || FreeQ[poleRatio, v | w | s] && \
MemberQ[{1, -1, I, -I}, Simplify[poleRatio/(2 (4 Pi)^(-2))]],\n  \"The \
virtual normalization is not 2/(4 Pi)^2 up to a phase\", \
poleRatio];\nvirtualHard = loopNormalization virtualSeries;\nvirtualPole2 = \
If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, -2}]];\n(* \
the universal double pole of a one-loop amplitude: -(alphaS/(2 Pi)) (sum of \
the Casimirs of the four partons) x Born *)\ncasimirSum = If[spec[\"Born\"] \
=== None, 0, Total[If[# === \"g\", CA, CF] & /@ \
spec[\"Born\"]]];\nRequire[virtualRaw === 0 || Simplify[(virtualPole2 + \
alphaS/(2 Pi) casimirSum born0) /. CF -> (CA^2 - 1)/(2 CA), region] === 0,\n  \
\"The virtual double pole is not -(alphaS/(2 Pi)) (C_a + C_b + C_c + C_d) \
times the Born term\", {virtualPole2, casimirSum born0}];\nvirtualPole1 = \
If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, \
-1}], region]];\nvirtualFinite = If[virtualRaw === 0, 0, \
Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[virtualHard, {eps, 0, 0}], \
region]];\nsymmetricVirtual = If[virtualRaw === 0, {0, 0, 0}, With[{ser = \
loopNormalization Series[virtualRawOf[\"Symmetric\"], {eps, 0, 0}]},\n  \
Table[Simplify[SeriesCoefficient[ser, {eps, 0, n}], region], {n, -2, \
0}]]];\nloopNormalization", "Input"], Cell["Counterterms", "Section"], 
  Cell["All counterterms multiply D-dimensional Born terms (through order \
eps) and carry the MSbar factor S_eps = (4 Pi)^eps e^(-eps EulerGamma) and \
the scale ratio (muR^2/mu^2)^eps of the subtraction: each gives a pole tuple \
K b0 and a finite tuple K (MSbarConstant b0 + b1 + log(muR^2/mu^2) b0), in \
the basis {regular, D0, delta}.  Splitting functions in units of alphaS/(2 \
Pi) are the kernels {regular(z), c0, cdelta} of \
FeynFacet/Factorization.wl.\n\nCoupling renormalization: -(alphaS/(2 Pi)) \
(beta0/eps) S_eps x Born with beta0 = (11 CA - 2 Nf)/3.\n\nIncoming leg a: + \
(alphaS/(2 Pi)) (1/eps) S_eps (muR^2/muF^2)^eps Delta_T P_qq convoluted with \
the TT Born of a b -> c (the transversity kernel connects the parton a only \
to itself: no sum over species), integral dz/z P(z) sigma_Born(z s, z t, u); \
the Born delta function fixes z = w and the convolution is P(w)/w times the \
Born hard part at (s, v), as a distribution [regular(w) + c0]/w + c0 \
[1/(1-w)]_+ + cdelta delta(1-w).\n\nIncoming leg b: the same with the Born of \
a b -> c at the rescaled parton b, integral dz/z P(z) sigma_Born(z s, t, z \
u); the Born delta function fixes z = z0 = (1-v)/(1-vw) and, as a \
distribution in w,\n\n   regular(z0) h(w) + c0 [g(w) - g(1)]/(1-w) + c0 g(1) \
D0(w) + [cdelta + c0 log(v/(1-v))] b0 delta(1-w),   h(w) = |M|^2(vw)/(16 Pi^2 \
s (1-v)),  g(w) = h(w) (1-vw)/v,  g(1) = b0,\n\nwhere |M|^2(vw) is the Born \
square at v -> vw (Processes/Hadronic derives and checks this pullback \
numerically).  Transversity enters the leg-b formula only through the kernel \
and the TT Born: the leg-exchanged (a <-> b) Born entries of the table are \
the t <-> u images of the originals (checked in step 2) and the pullback uses \
the class's own entry at the rescaled kinematics.\n\nFragmentation: + \
(alphaS/(2 Pi)) (1/eps) S_eps (muR^2/muFF^2)^eps Sum over d of the \
unpolarized P_{c <- d} convoluted with the TT Born of a b -> d at the \
rescaled final momentum, z = 1 - v + v w with the Jacobian v w/(1-v+vw)^2; \
for a kernel {regular, c0, cdelta} and j(w) the Born at the shifted point \
times the Jacobian,\n\n   (1/v) { c0 [j(w) - j(1)]/(1-w) + v regular(z) j(w), \
 c0 j(1),  j(1) [cdelta + c0 log v] },\n\nthe order-eps part of the Jacobian, \
z^(-2 eps), adding 2 log(z) j0 to the finite part.  A quark of flavour \
different from i (qp, qpb) stands for the Nf - 1 other flavours when it is \
summed over (class 12), exactly as in Processes/Hadronic.", "Text"], 
  Cell["quarkQ[x_] := x =!= \"g\";\n(* P_{c <- a} for the incoming legs: the \
transversity kernel, quark -> same quark only *)\nincomingKernel[cp_, a_] := \
If[quarkQ[a] && cp === a, SplittingKernel[\"Transverse qq\", z], {0, 0, \
0}];\n(* P_{c <- d} for the fragmenting parton: the unpolarized kernels \
*)\nkernel[c_, d_] := Which[\n  !quarkQ[c] && !quarkQ[d], \
SplittingKernel[\"gg\", z] /. {TR -> 1/2, nf -> Nf},\n  quarkQ[c] && \
quarkQ[d], If[c === d, SplittingKernel[\"qq\", z], {0, 0, 0}],\n  !quarkQ[c] \
&& quarkQ[d], SplittingKernel[\"gq\", z],\n  True, SplittingKernel[\"qg\", z] \
/. TR -> 1/2];\nspecies = {\"q\", \"qb\", \"qp\", \"qpb\", \"g\"};\n(* a \
quark of another flavour (qp, qpb) in a counterterm stands for Nf - 1 \
flavours when it is summed over, i.e. when the flavour k is not one of the\n  \
 external partons of the class (then it is the fixed flavour of that external \
parton); this matches the weights of the real-emission processes \
*)\nmultiplicity[x_, leg_] := If[MemberQ[{\"qp\", \"qpb\"}, x] && FreeQ[{a, \
b, c}, \"qp\" | \"qpb\"], Nf - 1, 1];\n{a, b, c} = If[spec[\"Born\"] =!= \
None, spec[\"Born\"][[1 ;; 3]], spec[\"Real\"][[1, 1]][[1 ;; 3]]];\n(* {b0, \
b1} of a Born square |M|^2/gs^4 (given in s, t) at the point v = vValue: \
|M|^2 (4 Pi alphaS)^2/(16 Pi^2 s vValue) with t = -s (1 - vValue), u = -s \
vValue *)\nbornPieces[square_, vValue_] := Module[{ser = Series[square (4 Pi \
alphaS)^2/(16 Pi^2 s vValue) /. D -> 4 - 2 eps /. {t -> -s (1 - vValue), u -> \
-s vValue}, {eps, 0, 1}]},\n  {SeriesCoefficient[ser, {eps, 0, 0}], \
SeriesCoefficient[ser, {eps, 0, 1}]}];\nbornAt[square_] := bornPieces[square, \
v];\nbeta0 = (11 CA - 2 Nf)/3;\nfiniteOf[K_List, {b0_, b1_}, logScale_] := K \
(MSbarConstant b0 + b1 + logScale b0);\nz0 = (1 - v)/(1 - v w);\ndw = 1 - v + \
v w;\njFunction[bornTerm_] := v w (bornTerm /. v -> v \
w/dw)/dw^2;\nfragmentationTuple[j_, k_] := Module[{jEnd = Simplify[j /. w -> \
1, region]},\n  (1/v) {k[[2]] (j - jEnd)/(1 - w) + v (k[[1]] /. z -> dw) j, \
k[[2]] jEnd, jEnd (k[[3]] + k[[2]] Log[v])}];\nzeroTuple = <|\"Pole\" -> {0, \
0, 0}, \"Finite\" -> {0, 0, 0}|>;\n\n(* the counterterms of one projection \
(TT, or Symmetric for the check) *)\ncountertermsOf[projection_] := \
Module[{renormalization, legA, legB, fragmentation},\n  renormalization = \
With[{bb = If[spec[\"Born\"] === None, {0, 0}, \
bornAt[algebra[\"Born\"][projection]/gs^4]]},\n    <|\"Pole\" -> {0, 0, \
-beta0 bb[[1]]}, \"Finite\" -> {0, 0, -beta0 (MSbarConstant bb[[1]] + \
bb[[2]])}|>];\n  (* leg a *)\n  legA = Total[Table[With[{k = \
incomingKernel[cp, a], square = bornOf[cp, b, c, projection]},\n    If[k === \
{0, 0, 0} || square === 0, zeroTuple,\n      With[{bb = bornAt[square], K = \
multiplicity[cp, a] {((k[[1]] /. z -> w) + k[[2]])/w, k[[2]], k[[3]]}},\n     \
   <|\"Pole\" -> K bb[[1]], \"Finite\" -> finiteOf[K, bb, \
Log[muR^2/muF^2]]|>]]], {cp, species}]];\n  (* leg b: the Born of a c' -> c \
at v -> v w, with the weights h and g *)\n  legB = Total[Table[With[{k = \
incomingKernel[cp, b], square = bornOf[a, cp, c, projection]},\n    If[k === \
{0, 0, 0} || square === 0, zeroTuple,\n      Module[{b0, b1, h0, h1, g0, g1, \
m = multiplicity[cp, b]},\n        {h0, h1} = bornPieces[square, v w] (v \
w)/(1 - v) // Simplify;            (* h(w) = |M|^2(v w)/(16 Pi^2 s (1-v)): \
the Born hard part at v w, with s v w replaced by s (1-v) *)\n        {g0, \
g1} = {h0, h1} (1 - v w)/v // Simplify;                             (* g(w); \
g(1) = b0 *)\n        {b0, b1} = bornAt[square];\n        \
Require[Simplify[(g0 /. w -> 1) - b0] === 0, \"g(1) differs from the Born\", \
{g0, b0}];\n        <|\"Pole\" -> m {(k[[1]] /. z -> z0) h0 + k[[2]] (g0 - \
b0)/(1 - w), k[[2]] b0, (k[[3]] + k[[2]] Log[v/(1 - v)]) b0},\n          \
\"Finite\" -> m {(k[[1]] /. z -> z0) (MSbarConstant h0 + h1 + \
Log[muR^2/muF^2] h0) + k[[2]] ((MSbarConstant g0 + g1 + Log[muR^2/muF^2] g0) \
- (MSbarConstant b0 + b1 + Log[muR^2/muF^2] b0))/(1 - w),\n            k[[2]] \
(MSbarConstant b0 + b1 + Log[muR^2/muF^2] b0), (k[[3]] + k[[2]] Log[v/(1 - \
v)]) (MSbarConstant b0 + b1 + Log[muR^2/muF^2] b0)}|>]]], {cp, species}]];\n  \
(* fragmentation *)\n  fragmentation = Total[Table[With[{k = kernel[c, d], \
square = bornOf[a, b, d, projection]},\n    If[k === {0, 0, 0} || square === \
0, zeroTuple,\n      With[{bb = bornAt[square]},\n        <|\"Pole\" -> \
multiplicity[d, c] fragmentationTuple[jFunction[bb[[1]]], k],\n          \
\"Finite\" -> multiplicity[d, c] fragmentationTuple[MSbarConstant \
jFunction[bb[[1]]] + jFunction[bb[[2]]] + 2 jFunction[bb[[1]]] Log[dw] + \
Log[muR^2/muFF^2] jFunction[bb[[1]]], k]|>]]], {d, species}]];\n  \
<|\"renormalization\" -> renormalization, \"incoming a\" -> legA, \"incoming \
b\" -> legB, \"fragmentation\" -> fragmentation|>];\ncounterterms = \
countertermsOf[spin];\nsymmetricCounterterms = \
countertermsOf[\"Symmetric\"];\nMap[Simplify[#, region] &, Map[#[\"Pole\"] &, \
counterterms]]", "Input"], Cell["Pole cancellation", "Section"], 
  Cell["The double poles of the real emission and the virtual correction \
cancel; the single poles of real, virtual and the four counterterms cancel in \
each of the three slots (regular, D0, delta).  SU(N) is needed: CF = \
(CA^2-1)/(2 CA).", "Text"], Cell["colourRelation = CF -> (CA^2 - 1)/(2 CA);   \
                   (* SU(N): the cancellations hold only with it \
*)\nRequire[Simplify[(realExpanded[\"Pole2\"] + virtualPole2) /. \
colourRelation, region] === 0, \"Double poles do not cancel\"];\npoleTable = \
Join[{realExpanded[\"Pole1\"], {0, 0, virtualPole1}}, alphaS/(2 Pi) \
Map[#[\"Pole\"] &, Values[counterterms]]];\nsinglePoles = Total[poleTable] /. \
reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"];        (* the pole coefficient of the mixed \
masters, F3[-1][z] = -2/(1-z), is a function of w *)\nRequire[And @@ \
(ExactDifference[# /. colourRelation, 0, region] === 0 & /@ singlePoles), \
\"Single poles do not cancel\", Simplify[singlePoles /. colourRelation, \
region]];\nGrid[Prepend[MapThread[Prepend[Map[Simplify[#, region] &, #2], #1] \
&,\n  {Join[{\"real emission\", \"virtual\"}, Keys[counterterms], {\"sum\"}], \
Append[poleTable, singlePoles]}],\n  {\"1/eps coefficient of\", \"regular\", \
\"D0\", \"delta\"}], Alignment -> Left, Dividers -> {{2 -> True}, {2 -> True, \
-2 -> True}}]", "Input"], Cell["The same for the symmetric projection.  Its \
Born term is of order eps, so the counterterms contribute no poles (their \
pole tuples are K b0 with b0 = 0), and the real and virtual parts must be \
finite by themselves after their double poles cancel: the whole structure of \
the evanescent part is tested here.  Its finite part (displayed in the next \
section) is a Phi-independent remainder of the HVBM scheme and does not \
belong to the physical cross section; it is not part of the result.", 
   "Text"], Cell["Require[Simplify[(symmetricReal[\"Pole2\"] + \
symmetricVirtual[[1]]) /. colourRelation, region] === 0, \"Double poles of \
the symmetric projection do not cancel\"];\nsymmetricPoleTable = \
Join[{symmetricReal[\"Pole1\"], {0, 0, symmetricVirtual[[2]]}}, alphaS/(2 Pi) \
Map[#[\"Pole\"] &, Values[symmetricCounterterms]]];\nsymmetricSinglePoles = \
Total[symmetricPoleTable] /. reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"];\nRequire[And @@ \
(ExactDifference[# /. colourRelation, 0, region] === 0 & /@ \
symmetricSinglePoles), \"Single poles of the symmetric projection do not \
cancel\", Simplify[symmetricSinglePoles /. colourRelation, region]];\n\"poles \
of the symmetric projection cancel\"", "Input"], 
  Cell["The finite hard coefficient", "Section"], 
  Cell["finiteCounterterms = alphaS/(2 Pi) Map[{#[\"Finite\"][[1]], \
#[\"Finite\"][[2]], 0, #[\"Finite\"][[3]]} &, counterterms];\nfinite = \
realExpanded[\"Finite\"] + {0, 0, 0, virtualFinite} + \
Total[Values[finiteCounterterms]];\nfinite = finite /. \
reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"];\nprefactor = alphaS^3/(Pi s v \
w);\nprefactorEndpoint = prefactor /. w -> 1;\ncoefficients = \
Map[Collect[Expand[#], {_Log, _PolyLog}, Factor] &, finite/{prefactor, \
prefactorEndpoint, prefactorEndpoint, \
prefactorEndpoint}];\nTableForm[Transpose[{ThresholdBasis, Map[Short[#, 6] &, \
coefficients]}]]", "Input"], Cell["symmetricFinite = \
(symmetricReal[\"Finite\"] + {0, 0, 0, symmetricVirtual[[3]]} + \
Total[Values[alphaS/(2 Pi) Map[{#[\"Finite\"][[1]], #[\"Finite\"][[2]], 0, \
#[\"Finite\"][[3]]} &, symmetricCounterterms]]]) /. \
reduced[\"MasterFunctions\"];\nsymmetricCoefficients = Map[Simplify[# /. \
colourRelation, region] &, symmetricFinite/{prefactor, prefactorEndpoint, \
prefactorEndpoint, prefactorEndpoint}];\n\"finite Phi-independent remainder \
of the HVBM scheme (not part of the result), coefficients {regular, D0, D1, \
delta}\" -> symmetricCoefficients", "Input"], Cell["Scale dependence", 
   "Section"], Cell["As in Processes/Collins/5-Transverse.wl: the derivatives \
of the finite coefficient with respect to the three scales are fixed by the \
renormalization group, mu^2 d/dmu^2 of the finite coefficient equals minus \
the corresponding pole tuple (in units of alphaS/(2 Pi)): for muF the \
transversity kernel convoluted with the Born on both incoming legs, for muFF \
the unpolarized kernels convoluted with the Born table for the fragmenting \
parton, for muR the beta0 term, (alphaS/(2 Pi)) beta0 x Born in the \
delta(1-w) slot.  The muR check uses the muR^(2 eps) of the real and virtual \
parts together with the counterterm logarithms and is a consequence of the \
pole cancellation; all three are required.", "Text"], 
  Cell["scaleDerivative[mu_] := mu/2 D[finite, mu];                           \
        (* mu^2 d/dmu^2 *)\ntuple4[t3_List] := {t3[[1]], t3[[2]], 0, \
t3[[3]]};\nscaleChecks = <|\n  \"muF\" -> Simplify[scaleDerivative[muF] + \
alphaS/(2 Pi) tuple4[counterterms[\"incoming a\"][\"Pole\"] + \
counterterms[\"incoming b\"][\"Pole\"]], region],\n  \"muFF\" -> \
Simplify[scaleDerivative[muFF] + alphaS/(2 Pi) \
tuple4[counterterms[\"fragmentation\"][\"Pole\"]], region],\n  \"muR\" -> \
Simplify[(scaleDerivative[muR] + alphaS/(2 Pi) \
tuple4[counterterms[\"renormalization\"][\"Pole\"]]) /. colourRelation, \
region]|>;\nRequire[And @@ (ExactDifference[#, 0, region] === 0 & /@ \
Flatten[Values[scaleChecks]]), \"A scale derivative is not the corresponding \
splitting convolution (or the beta0 term)\", scaleChecks];\n\"scale \
dependence verified: muF (transversity kernel on both legs), muFF \
(unpolarized kernels), muR (beta0)\"", "Input"], 
  Cell["Comparison with the reference code", "Section"], 
  Cell["Reference/pp-transverse-fortran.wl transcribes the pieces of the \
Fortran code of Mukherjee, Stratmann and Vogelsang (me-transpol-ms.f) for \
this class as exact expressions in v, w, s, the three scales, CA, CF and Nf, \
and PPTransverseFortranCoefficients[J0] maps them onto this basis (the map, \
fixed on the Born term of class 5, is explained in the header of that file \
and in Reference/fortran/README.md: the code's slots hold one half of the \
cos(2 Phi) coefficient, so {R, B, C, A} = 2 alphaS^3/(Pi s CC) {STRUV, \
AVWPL(1), AVLO(1), AVDEL} and Born = 2 (1-v) F0 alphaS^2/Pi).  Both sides \
being exact, they are compared at exact rational points with 30 digits: \
several (v, w), distinct values of the three scales, two values of Nf (the \
dependence is linear), at SU(3) and at other values of CA with CF = \
(CA^2-1)/(2 CA).  The relative difference of each coefficient is taken with \
respect to its reference value plus 1/100 of the largest of the four at that \
point (a coefficient that nearly vanishes at a point is compared at absolute \
precision, as in Processes/Hadronic).  The SU(3) comparison is required; the \
general-colour one is reported (the code was written for SU(3) and the \
transcription cannot decide whether its CA, CF dependence is the general \
one).", "Text"], Cell["fortranFile = FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"..\", \"..\", \"Reference\", \
\"pp-transverse-fortran.wl\"}];\ncomparisonPoints = Flatten[Table[Join[{v -> \
vw[[1]], w -> vw[[2]], s -> 7/5, muR -> Sqrt[9/10], muF -> Sqrt[7/10], muFF \
-> Sqrt[13/10], Nf -> nf, alphaS -> 1}, colour],\n  {vw, {{3/10, 1/5}, \
{11/20, 3/5}, {4/5, 9/10}, {1/2, 1/2}, {2/5, 7/10}, {19/20, 1/20}}}, {nf, {4, \
5}},\n  {colour, {{CA -> 3, CF -> 4/3}, {CA -> 2, CF -> 3/4}, {CA -> 5, CF -> \
12/5}}}], 2];\nrelative[o_, t_] := With[{scale = Max[Abs[t]]/100}, \
MapThread[If[Abs[#1] < 10^-25 && Abs[#2] < 10^-25, 0, (#1 - #2)/(Abs[#2] + \
scale)] &, {o, t}]];\nIf[!FileExistsQ[fortranFile],\n  \
Print[\"Reference/pp-transverse-fortran.wl is not available yet: the \
comparison with the reference code is skipped\"]; comparison = None,\n  \
Get[fortranFile];\n  theirs = \
PPTransverseFortranCoefficients[ToExpression[class]];\n  theirCoefficients = \
{theirs[\"Regular\"], theirs[\"D0\"], theirs[\"D1\"], theirs[\"Delta\"]};\n  \
comparison = Table[With[{o = N[coefficients /. point, 30], th = \
N[theirCoefficients /. point, 30]},\n    <|\"v\" -> (v /. point), \"w\" -> (w \
/. point), \"Nf\" -> (Nf /. point), \"CA\" -> (CA /. point), \"ours\" -> o, \
\"theirs\" -> th, \"relative\" -> relative[o, th],\n      \"Born\" -> \
N[(born0 - theirs[\"Born\"]) /. point, 30]|>], {point, comparisonPoints}];\n  \
su3 = Select[comparison, #[\"CA\"] == 3 &]; general = Select[comparison, \
#[\"CA\"] != 3 &];\n  Require[Max[Abs[Lookup[su3, \"Born\"]]] < 10^-20, \"The \
Born term differs from the reference code\", Lookup[su3, \"Born\"]];\n  \
Require[Max[Abs[Flatten[Lookup[su3, \"relative\"]]]] < 10^-20, \"The hard \
coefficient differs from the reference code at SU(3)\", Grid[Map[{#[\"v\"], \
#[\"w\"], #[\"Nf\"], NumberForm[#[\"relative\"], 3]} &, su3]]];\n  \
Print[\"largest relative difference at SU(3): \", Max[Abs[Flatten[Lookup[su3, \
\"relative\"]]]], \"; at CA = 2 and 5: \", Max[Abs[Flatten[Lookup[general, \
\"relative\"]]]]]];\nIf[comparison =!= None, Grid[Prepend[Map[{#[\"v\"], \
#[\"w\"], #[\"Nf\"], #[\"CA\"], NumberForm[#[\"relative\"], 3]} &, \
comparison], {\"v\", \"w\", \"Nf\", \"CA\", \"differences of {R, B, C, A} \
(relative, see above)\"}], Frame -> All]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Save", "Section"], Cell["result = <|\"Basis\" -> ThresholdBasis, \
\"Prefactor\" -> prefactor, \"Coefficients\" -> coefficients, \"Finite\" -> \
finite, \"Born\" -> born0, \"Virtual\" -> virtualFinite,\n  \"Real\" -> \
realExpanded[\"Finite\"], \"Counterterms\" -> finiteCounterterms, \
\"SymmetricRemainder\" -> symmetricCoefficients, \"ScaleChecks\" -> \
scaleChecks,\n  \"Comparison\" -> comparison, \"Route\" -> route, \"Class\" \
-> class, \"Spin\" -> spin,\n  \"Observable\" -> \"delta sigma_TT = \
(1/2)[sigma(up,up) - sigma(up,down)] projected on cos(2 Phi); sigma = s E \
dsigma/d^3p; scales muR, muF (both transversity densities), \
muFF\"|>;\nSaveResult[\"Transverse\", result, route <> \"/class-\" <> \
class];\nKeys[result]", "Input"]}, WindowSize -> {1000, 800}]
